One read-only reviewer on the teardown instrument and the `0x491B60` caller
survey, asked to re-derive every binary claim from pristine/TotalA.exe.pristine
rather than take it from the diff. Acted on in 88cb8ec and 412d309.

IT FOUND THE SURVEY'S HEADLINE FALSE, and two source files plus two notes had
already begun citing it.

 F1 HIGH  "three of the six call sites are DEAD CODE in 0x499100" is wrong.
          0x499100 is an ordinary stdcall helper 0x499100..0x4991FB (ret 4,
          then nops) containing no call to the teardown; the three calls are
          in 0x499200, a LIVE state handler, address-taken at 0x490BC5 and
          0x498455 and dispatched through [main+0x391F5] at 0x499A1C. By its
          behaviour it is the quit and restart paths -- so the commit measured
          four teardowns arriving through sites it had just called unreachable.
          VERIFIED independently before acting: the function boundary by
          objdump, the two address-taken sites by byte scan (file 0x8FFCB,
          0x9785B), the dispatcher by objdump.
 F2 HIGH  three of the four "in function" attributions wrong the same way
          (0x4604A0 for 0x4605C0, 0x491EC0 for 0x492360), and a six-hop chain
          of "direct calls" that does not connect. CAUSE: call sites were
          attributed to the nearest preceding E8 target, which is blind to
          functions that are only ever dispatched through a pointer -- and in
          this binary that is every main-loop function. Written into the note
          as the method lesson, which is the part worth keeping.
 F3 MED   "the loader has no control path of any kind" is a direct-call result
          only; that closure holds 690 indirect call sites. Restated.
 F4 MED   char b[200] for a line whose worst case is 239. msvcrt's _snprintf
          writes _Count chars, returns -1 and does NOT terminate on truncation,
          and rlog would read past it. Fits on Wine's 3-digit tids, which is
          why the daily loop would never show it. Now b[320] + explicit
          termination. VERIFIED by recomputing the format width.
 F5 MED   the FOREIGN suffix tested the CUMULATIVE counter, so one foreign
          teardown would mark every later game-thread one FOREIGN -- the exact
          failure mode 9b0bcde added its baseline guard to prevent. Now this
          teardown's verdict; cumulative stays on the heartbeat.
 F6 LOW   "three threads exist" -> "three thread-creation call sites"
          (0x4E77D0 is a generic CRT spawn wrapper).
 F7 LOW   the stated scan method omitted 70-7F, E3, E0-E2. Result unchanged;
          the claim that depended on it is withdrawn anyway.
 F8 LOW   "can never fire" absolutes on the two golden-source guards, and the
          reclaim/packet_pub baseline difference (gui_thread_id vs s_gameTid).
          Both stated.

AND CHASING F1 TO ITS END FOUND THE ARGUMENT THAT WAS ACTUALLY THERE (412d309,
after the review): 0x499200 calls the frame callback 0x496790 directly at
0x4995B8 and 0x4996A5 -- the latter the instruction immediately before the
teardown call at 0x4996AA, same straight line -- and 0x496790 is what the
publisher gates with on_game_thread() on every in-play draw, never once
foreign. So three of the six sites are pinned by construction after all, just
not the three the first version claimed. The other three rest on the four
measured teardowns plus the tdforeign= instrument, and the note records that
the measured teardowns went through the pinned three, so the unpinned three
are the least exercised.

CONFIRMED by the reviewer against the binary: 0x00491B60 appears at no offset
of the 1 178 624-byte image and there is no .reloc, so the entry set is closed;
exactly six E8 calls at the six stated addresses (scan is a superset, so both
bounds); exactly one control transfer into 0x499100, over E8/E9/EB/0F8x plus
70-7F/E3/E0-E2; CreateThread the only thread-creation import with three call
sites; the loader's identity 0x4982C5 -> 0x4B6B20 -> 0x4E77D0 -> CreateThread,
entry 0x497C70 -> body 0x497180; and tagpu_packet_pub.c's new claim that
after_teardown is unreachable off the game thread (before returns 0, and
tagpu_detour.c:181-190 emits jz +8 past the return hijack).
CONFIRMED on the instrument: both new statics are naturally-aligned 32-bit
words so no torn read is possible, nothing branches on either, staleness is
bounded by one heartbeat and can only delay a report; and no state exists in
which g_ddraw.gui_thread_id is non-zero but names a thread other than the
game thread (single write in dd_SetCooperativeLevel, from
GetWindowThreadProcessId, never cleared).

